  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Ramularia rubella is a fungal pathogen belonging to the Mycosphaerellaceae family that causes leaf spots on various plant hosts in New England regions. This ascomycete fungus is economically significant as it affects crop and ornamental plant health through foliar infections.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Reddish-brown leaf spots",
      "description": "Circular to angular necrotic lesions with distinctive reddish-brown coloration, often with a darker border and lighter center",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Conidiophores and conidia",
      "description": "Microscopic examination reveals simple, unbranched conidiophores bearing small, typically one to two-celled hyaline conidia",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Lesion progression",
      "description": "Spots may coalesce during wet conditions, leading to larger areas of blighted foliage and potential defoliation",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Ramularia rubella thrives in moist, cool conditions typical of New England springs and falls, preferring hosts in open gardens and agricultural settings with high humidity.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "This fungus is one of several Ramularia species that can produce both anamorphic (asexual) and teleomorphic (sexual) stages, with some species having associated Mycosphaerella sexual states.",
